course: dictionary
lesson: 3819
title: Forgivable mistakes
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A mistake is forgivable when it is small enough for people to pardon. The word is made from forgive and -able, so it means able to be forgiven. «One accident is forgivable, but a pattern of excuses, like "losing" a borrowed thing three times, becomes a trust problem.» The word accident is a clue: it means something went wrong without anyone meaning it.
explain: The sentence shows that one mistake and a repeated pattern are not the same. Losing a borrowed thing once can be forgiven. Losing it three times, with an excuse each time, is much harder to pardon. Ask yourself: is this mistake small enough to excuse?
explain: Test your meaning by swapping it in: "The accident was forgivable" means the accident could be forgiven. Phrases such as a forgivable mistake, perfectly forgivable and easily forgivable show you how the word is used.
fact: Forgivable means able to be forgiven, or not so bad that you cannot excuse it.
fact: «Forgivable» is an adjective, and its comparative form is more forgivable.
fact: A forgivable mistake is one that people can pardon, such as a small accident.
fact: «One accident is forgivable» means one accident can be pardoned.
fact: Repeated mistakes, such as a pattern of excuses, can make a problem harder to forgive.
exercise: «One accident is forgivable, but a pattern of excuses becomes a trust problem.» What does forgivable mean? | Very rare and strange | *Able to be forgiven | Loud and noisy | Impossible to explain
exercise: Which mistake is most likely to be forgivable? | Hurting a friend on purpose and laughing about it | *Knocking over a cup by accident | Ignoring a friend who asks for help | Telling the same lie again and again
exercise: «One accident is forgivable, but a pattern of excuses becomes a trust problem.» Find and copy the word that shows the mistake was not meant. | pattern | trust | *accident | excuses
test: The word forgivable is closest in meaning to which phrase? | Hard to see in the dark | Dangerous and wild | Very loud and noisy | *Not so bad that you cannot excuse it
test: Which phrase from the lessons means a mistake that can be pardoned? | a pattern of excuses | *a forgivable mistake | a borrowed thing | a trust problem
exam: Why does a pattern of excuses make a mistake harder to forgive? | *Because repeated excuses show that trust is breaking down | Because forgivable means rare | Because borrowed things are always expensive | Because three is an unlucky number
